==Albanian==

===Etymology 1===
Probably from {{etyl|ine-pro|sq}} {{recons|mendh|lang=ine-pro}} 'to pay attention to, be vivacious'. Compare Old Norse {{term|munda||aim, strive|lang=non}}, Gothic {{term|mundon||look up|lang=got}}, Old High German {{term|muntar||keen, eager|lang=goh}}, Ancient Greek {{term|μανθάνω||learn|lang=grc}}, Lithuanian {{term|mañdras||alert, awake, smart, minxish|lang=lt}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/mʊnd/|lang=sq}}

====Noun====
{{head|sq|noun|g=m}}

# [[trouble]]
# [[toil]]

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|sq|mundur}}
* {{l|sq|mundje}}
* {{l|sq|mundim}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|sq|Muji}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|sqj-pro|sq}} {{recons|ma̅K(e)nT-|lang=sqj-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|sq}} {{recons|meh2K-|lang=ine-pro}} 'can, to be able (to do)'. Cognate to Lithuanian {{term|mokė́ti||to be able|lang=lt}}, Gothic {{term|magan||to be able, have power|lang=got}}, Old Church Slavic {{term|mogǫ||to be able|lang=cu}}. Alternatively from {{etyl|ine-pro|sq}} {{recons|men(s)-dʰ(e)h₂|lang=ine-pro}} ‘to learn’.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/mʊnd/|lang=sq}}

====Verb====
{{sq-verb|munda|mundur}}

# I [[can]].
# I am [[able]].
# I [[beat]], [[win]] over.

=====Inflection=====
{{sq-conj-c-a-ur|mund}}

=====Related terms=====
* [[mundem]]

